  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Minburn Technology Group obligated $767M in federal contracts in FY2026 to date across 453 awards, with an average award size of $1.7M and obligations down 18.9% year over year. Minburn recorded higher obligations in prior years, including $946M in FY25 and $927M in FY24, after rising from $559M in FY21 and $494M in FY22 and peaking at $796M in FY23. The General Services Administration is the largest buyer in FY2026 to date with $261M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $143M and the Department of State with $140M. The largest markets by NAICS are Other Computer Related Services at $396M and Software Publishers at $352M, with additional activity in Computing Infrastructure Providers, Data Processing, Web Hosting, and Related Services at $11.1M and several hardware and manufacturing categories at smaller dollar levels. Current-year totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Minburn Technology Group's FY2026 federal obligations?",
        "body": "Minburn Technology Group's FY2026 obligations show concentration among a few large buyers and markets. The General Services Administration accounts for $261M, and the next two largest buyers are the Department of Veterans Affairs at $143M and the Department of State at $140M. The largest NAICS markets are Other Computer Related Services at $396M and Software Publishers at $352M. Several other NAICS categories and agencies represent much smaller dollar amounts in FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Minburn Technology Group's main customers and what do they buy?",
        "body": "The General Services Administration, Department of Veterans Affairs, and Department of State are Minburn's top buying agencies in FY2026 to date at $261M, $143M, and $140M respectively. Federal customers are buying software and computer services, led by Other Computer Related Services at $396M and Software Publishers at $352M. Agencies also purchased computing infrastructure, data processing, and web hosting services at $11.1M, plus smaller amounts for computer hardware and manufacturing categories."
      }
    ]
